  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalled front currently stretches from the TN\nValley east through ENC. Along the front resides a very moist \nairmass with PWATs of 2\"+, and modest instability with MLCAPE of\n500- 1000j/kg. Over the next 24 hours, this front is forecast \nto wabble some as various mid-level waves ride along it, but \ngenerally remain locked in place over ENC. While periods of rain\nare expected to persist along the front, the intensity of the \nrain will fluctuate, peaking with each passing wave. One wave is\nmoving through now, and the next wave is forecast to move \nthrough later this evening and tonight. There may be a bit of a \n\"lull\" in rainfall coverage and intensity on Friday as the front\nsags south temporarily. The front should then get pulled back \nnorth Friday night into Saturday as yet another wave traverse \nthe frontal zone. Each successive wave is forecast to have less \ninstability with it, which is one factor that should limit the \nduration of the more intense rainfall rates. However, given the \nanomalous moisture present, plus increased low- level and large-\nscale forcing, rainfall rates of 0.50\"-1.50\" should be common \nfor several hours through tonight, and again later Friday \nthrough Saturday morning.\n\nThis should support additional rainfall totals of 2-4\", with \nhigher amounts where the deepest/most persistent convection \noccurs. Because of the waning instability and lowering rainfall \nrates, the risk of flash flooding has slightly decreased. The \nlatest excessive rainfall outlook from WPC now shows a Slight \nRisk of flash flooding for ENC through Saturday. \n\nThe passage of earlier convection has stabilized the atms over\nENC for a while, and thus have lowered the prob for\nthunderstorms to 20-30%. On Friday, best chances of\nthunderstorms are generally near and to the south of where \ninstability is the highest (ie. south of HWY 70).",
